    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. Only `AcceptanceTest` is a runnable test class (the other two files are the base class and a capture helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added the daily-limit rule to `POST /api/owners` in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`:\n\n```java\nif (owners.stream()\n    .filter(o -> java.time.LocalDate.now().equals(o.getRegistrationDate())).count() >= 100) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);\n}\n```\n\n**Design for low structural impact:**\n- It reuses the `owners` collection already fetched by `findAllOwners()` a line earlier \u2014 no extra service call or query.\n- It's a single 3-line guard inside the one method that must enforce it, touching **one file** and adding just one branch. Both an inline check and a helper-class approach require the same new `if` in `addOwner` (so `addOwner`'s cyclomatic complexity rises identically either way), but a helper would add a second file \u2014 and since the cost is multiplied by the number of files touched, the inline form is strictly cheaper here.\n- No change to the `Owner` model, mapper, or service layer; `getRegistrationDate()` and the `@PrePersist` default already exist.\n\nWhen 100 or more existing owners carry today's `registrationDate`, the endpoint now responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`.",
